Ok. Let&#39;s say I&#39;m working with a simple vector &quot;names&quot;, which has the<br>following elements:<br><br>[1] &quot;James&quot;    &quot;Jonathan&quot; &quot;Jean-Luc&quot;<br><br>I want to print this to a text file saved n my hard drive<br><br>Here&#39;s the script I&#39;m using at the suggestion of the book:<br><br>&gt;cat(&quot;names&quot;, choose.files(), sep=&quot;
&quot;)<br><br>Supposing I select the file: 03-2-4_vector-a  in the dialogue box, R<br>prints:<br><br>names<br>C:\_qclwr\_outputfiles\03-2-4_vector-a.txt<br><br>But no change is made to the file. If I paste the file name into the<br>script so we see something like this:<br><br><br>&gt;cat(&quot;names&quot;, file=&quot;C:\_qclwr\_outputfiles\03-2-4_vector-a.txt&quot;, sep=&quot;
&quot;)<br><br>I get the following doomsday message:<br><br>Error in file(file, ifelse(append, &quot;a&quot;, &quot;w&quot;)) :<br>  cannot open the connection<br>In addition: Warning messages:<br>1: &#39;\_&#39; is an unrecognized escape in a character string<br>2: &#39;\_&#39; is an unrecognized escape in a character string<br>3: unrecognized escapes removed from &quot;C:\_qclwr\_outputfiles<br>\0-2-4_vector-a.txt&quot;<br>4: In file(file, ifelse(append, &quot;a&quot;, &quot;w&quot;)) :<br>  cannot open file &#39;C:_qclwr_outputfiles -2-4_vector-a.txt&#39;: Invalid<br>argument<br><br>I looked through the R manual, and it suggested that I should double<br>the backslashes. So no we&#39;ve got this:<br><br>&gt;cat(&quot;names&quot;, file=&quot;C:\_qclwr\_outputfiles\03-2-4_vector-a.txt&quot;, sep=&quot;
&quot;)<br><br>R returns just the prompt:<br><br>&gt;<br><br>leading me to believe I&#39;ve been successful. But no. The text file<br>contains only the vector name &quot;names&quot;, and not the elements of the<br>vector.<br>

Hi<p>You have to write<p>cat(&quot;names&quot;, file=choose.files(), sep=&quot;
&quot;) # cat(&quot;names&quot;,<br>choose.files(), sep=&quot;
&quot;)<p>Otherwise, R thinks the string resulting from choose.files() is<br>another string to be printed, as in<p>cat(1:3, 4:6, sep=&quot;
&quot;)<p>That is, it lets you select a file name and prints it out. If you use<br>the above, R knows that the string resulting from choose.files() is<br>meant to be cat&#39;s file argument.<p>Cheers,<br>STG<br>--<br>Stefan Th. Gries<br>-----------------------------------------------<br>University of California, Santa Barbara<br><a href="http://www.linguistics.ucsb.edu/faculty/stgries" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.linguistics.ucsb.edu/faculty/stgries&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792492407573000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2fV3rOnjudRPEhlmMDGz_U">http://www.linguistics.ucsb.edu/faculty/stgries</a><br>-----------------------------------------------<br></p></p></p></p></p></p>
